select '2026-08-02.1', 'PARA-0006', 'NEIGHBOURHOOD_REPAIR_CAF_0006', 'Neighbourhood Repair Café', 'Community / Sustainability', 'B1–B2', 'Despite of receiving only a small grant, our neighbourhood repair café has become one of the most useful project in the district. It has operated since six months and used to offering help only with lamps, but it now accepts bicycles, radios and small kitchen machines. Many residents look forward to learn simple skills instead of throwing damaged objects away. Last Saturday, the volunteers managed fixing twenty items and prevented several batteries ending up in the rubbish. At the entrance, a coordinator carefully explained new visitors the safety rules and provided every team by the necessary gloves. She also reminded to write their names on a form before entering the workshop. Parents were happy because the organisers let children to test safe tools, although they made everyone to wear eye protection. One instructor advised avoiding to touch loose wires and suggested considering to replace any cracked plug. Another volunteer insisted checking each cable twice and said that he was responsible recording every repair. Most visitors prefer repairing old things than buying new ones, and several said they would rather to donate unused tools than throwing them away. The printed instructions were enough clear for beginners to follow, and the final demonstration was such practical that nobody became confused. At closing time, two students were responsible for both sorting spare parts and to clean the tables. Each visitor later chose between taking the item home or leaving it for collection. Between my neighbour and I, we carried the volunteers tools to a cupboard beside a resident who''s bicycle had been repaired. The amount of volunteers was encouraging, while the others participants promised to return. By the time the doors closed, the final guest already left, the coordinator still had three forms to check before the next community event in early autumn.', 'Despite receiving only a small grant, our neighbourhood repair café has become one of the most useful projects in the district. It has operated for six months and used to offer help only with lamps, but it now accepts bicycles, radios and small kitchen machines. Many residents look forward to learning simple skills instead of throwing damaged objects away. Last Saturday, the volunteers managed to fix twenty items and prevented several batteries from ending up in the rubbish. At the entrance, a coordinator carefully explained the safety rules to new visitors and provided every team with the necessary gloves. She also reminded them to write their names on a form before entering the workshop. Parents were happy because the organisers let children test safe tools, although they made everyone wear eye protection. One instructor advised avoiding touching loose wires and suggested considering replacing any cracked plug. Another volunteer insisted on checking each cable twice and said that he was responsible for recording every repair. Most visitors prefer repairing old things to buying new ones, and several said they would rather donate unused tools than throw them away. The printed instructions were clear enough for beginners to follow, and the final demonstration was so practical that nobody became confused. At closing time, two students were responsible for both sorting spare parts and cleaning the tables. Each visitor later chose between taking the item home and leaving it for collection. Between my neighbour and me, we carried the volunteers'' tools to a cupboard beside a resident whose bicycle had been repaired. The number of volunteers was encouraging, while the other participants promised to return. By the time the doors closed, the final guest had already left; the coordinator still had three forms to check before the next community event in early autumn.', 300, 300, 16, 29, 'British English', 'Edmund Sir', 'approved', 1, false, false, 'Dataset 6 from the supplied Grammar Dataset 3 to 18 PDF. Approved for canonical storage in the development partition; excluded from live retrieval pending a separate production review.'
where exists (select 1 from public.writing_grammar_corpus_releases where corpus_version = '2026-08-02.1' and status = 'reviewed') on conflict do nothing;
insert into public.writing_grammar_paragraphs (corpus_version, paragraph_id, group_key, title, topic_category, student_level, incorrect_paragraph, corrected_paragraph, original_word_count, corrected_word_count, sentence_count, issue_count, english_variant, author, status, version, retrieval_eligible, evaluation_holdout, notes)
select '2026-08-02.1', 'PARA-0007', 'MUNICIPAL_DRAINAGE_ACCOUNTABILITY_REVIEW_0007', 'Municipal Drainage Accountability Review', 'Government / Infrastructure / Public Accountability', 'B2–C1', 'Following last winter’s floods, the council set out a panel to look into on why the drainage programme had fallen beneath schedule. The chair asked engineers to follow on every complaint and come up to a plan that would account about the failures. Not only several contractors failed to comply to the safety code, and they also tried to cover up about delays that should have been reported earlier. No sooner the investigators had drawn up on a timetable when one supplier pulled off of the project. Only after the financial records had been examined the panel ruled out against fraud. What it concerned residents most was that officials had put off replace pumps on that several low-lying districts depended. The committee recommended that each contractor provides monthly evidence and that emergency drills carried out twice a year. It is essential that the revised system is tested before the rainy season began, lest another breakdown leaves families without assistance. If had the council acted sooner, it might have prevented damage that is believed having cost local businesses millions. Much although the mayor wanted to defend the original scheme, she admitted that the authority had failed to live up with its promises. Residents would rather the council publishes all future reports than withholding inconvenient findings. By next June, the new monitoring team will take over of the temporary inspectors. However the repairs complicated may become, officials must deal about them transparently. Having reviewed the evidence, the conclusion was that what the city needs is not another short-term campaign but a permanent maintenance strategy. The more consistently agencies work together, similar failures are the less likely to recur. Until all recommendations will be implemented, no department can take it as granted that public confidence will simply return without sustained effort or scrutiny.', 'Following last winter’s floods, the council set up a panel to look into why the drainage programme had fallen behind schedule. The chair asked engineers to follow up on every complaint and come up with a plan that would account for the failures. Not only did several contractors fail to comply with the safety code, but they also tried to cover up delays that should have been reported earlier. No sooner had the investigators drawn up a timetable than one supplier pulled out of the project. Only after the financial records had been examined did the panel rule out fraud. What concerned residents most was that officials had put off replacing pumps on which several low-lying districts depended. The committee recommended that each contractor provide monthly evidence and that emergency drills be carried out twice a year. It is essential that the revised system be tested before the rainy season begins, lest another breakdown leave families without assistance. Had the council acted sooner, it might have prevented damage that is believed to have cost local businesses millions. Much as the mayor wanted to defend the original scheme, she admitted that the authority had failed to live up to its promises. Residents would rather the council published all future reports than withheld inconvenient findings. By next June, the new monitoring team will have taken over from the temporary inspectors. However complicated the repairs may become, officials must deal with them transparently. Having reviewed the evidence, the panel concluded that what the city needs is not another short-term campaign but a permanent maintenance strategy. The more consistently agencies work together, the less likely similar failures are to recur. Until all recommendations have been implemented, no department can take it for granted that public confidence will simply return without sustained effort or scrutiny.', 300, 300, 16, 38, 'British English', 'Edmund Sir', 'approved', 1, false, false, 'Dataset 7 from the supplied Grammar Dataset 3 to 18 PDF. Approved for canonical storage in the development partition; excluded from live retrieval pending a separate production review.'
