select '2026-08-02.1', 'PARA-0008', 'PREVENTIVE_HEALTH_POLICY_0008', 'Preventive Health Policy', 'Public Health / Government Policy', 'B2–C1', 'Good health policy should not begin at the hospital door. I completely agree with preventing illness is more important to treating it after it has already developed, and public funding should therefore give the strongest priority for prevention. The first reason is that prevention saves both money as human suffering. When governments invest on health education, vaccination programmes, early screening, safe parks, and better nutrition in schools, many serious conditions can stop before they become expensive medicine crises. A health system, that only responds after people are already sick, is always fighting fires: hospitals become crowded, doctors are overstretched, and taxpayers must pay long-term treatment that might have avoided. For example, if children are taught healthy eating habits and are given at places to exercise, they are less likely of developing obesity-related illnesses later at life. This means that fewer patients needing complex surgery, lifelong medication, or repeated hospital visits. Prevention, therefore, does not merely reduce costs; it protects people of pain that no medicine can fully erase it. Another reason is due to preventive spending creates a fairer society. Treatment often reaches people only after damage has done, whereas that prevention can protect whole communities before illness takes its root. This is especially important for poorer families, which may lack of access for nutritious food, safe housing, mental-health support, or regular medical checks. If public money is used to improve air quality, providing free health screenings, support school meals, and promote active lifestyles, the benefits are shared wide rather than reserve for those whom can afford for private care. In this sense, prevention is not just a medical strategy; it is a social investment that helps citizens remain healthily, productivity, and independently. In conclusion, governments should clearly prioritise on the prevention of disease because of it reduces avoidable suffering, lowering pressure to healthcare systems, and protects society more equally. Although treating illness matters, but preventing it is the more wiser use for public money.', 'Good health policy should not begin at the hospital door. I completely agree that preventing illness is more important than treating it after it has already developed, and public funding should therefore give prevention the strongest priority. The first reason is that prevention saves both money and human suffering. When governments invest in health education, vaccination programmes, early screening, safe parks, and better nutrition in schools, many serious conditions can be stopped before they become expensive medical crises. A health system that only responds after people are already sick is always fighting fires: hospitals become crowded, doctors are overstretched, and taxpayers must pay for long-term treatment that might have been avoided. For example, if children are taught healthy eating habits and are given places to exercise, they are less likely to develop obesity-related illnesses later in life. This means fewer patients needing complex surgery, lifelong medication, or repeated hospital visits. Prevention, therefore, does not merely reduce costs; it protects people from pain that no medicine can fully erase. Another reason is that preventive spending creates a fairer society. Treatment often reaches people only after damage has been done, whereas prevention can protect whole communities before illness takes root. This is especially important for poorer families, who may lack access to nutritious food, safe housing, mental-health support, or regular medical checks. If public money is used to improve air quality, provide free health screenings, support school meals, and promote active lifestyles, the benefits are shared widely rather than reserved for those who can afford private care. In this sense, prevention is not just a medical strategy; it is a social investment that helps citizens remain healthy, productive, and independent. In conclusion, governments should clearly prioritise the prevention of disease because it reduces avoidable suffering, lowers pressure on healthcare systems, and protects society more equally. Treating illness matters, but preventing it is the wiser use of public money.', 325, 316, 15, 36, 'British English', 'Edmund Sir', 'approved', 1, false, false, 'Dataset 8 from the supplied Grammar Dataset 3 to 18 PDF. Approved for canonical storage in the development partition; excluded from live retrieval pending a separate production review.'
