select '2026-08-02.1', 'PARA-0009', 'HOLIDAY_OVERWORK_AND_EMPLOYEE_WELL_BEING_0009', 'Holiday Overwork and Employee Well-being', 'Employment / Work–life Balance / Health', 'B1–B2', 'Nowadays, many employers blame on the phenomenon of overwork during holiday as their employers offer jobs for them apart from working hours. I highly believe that this problem has placed detrimental effects on workers, in terms of workloads, stress and social relationships. The most significant problem is that workers may suffer from higher workloads. As employers are able to connect with them at all times, they can offer job duties and request on urgent projects. As such, employers may sacrifice their holiday and take extra amount of work. Besides, working on holidays imposes high pressure on employees. Due to the fact that workers have to stay alert for phone calls from employers, they may devote less time on relaxation and be prepared for the request. As a result, they have less chance to relive their stress during holiday, and which may boost their anxiety under stress. Moreover, employees who need to work 247 may spend less time with their friends or partners. In addition to family gathering, employers who have to stay behind for work may devote less time in social activities. This generally destroys their social bonding with peers and draws on negative effects to their social relationships. However, some may judge that being connected with employers enhances their working efficiency. Workers who have difficulties can ask for advice from their employers and to receive immediate responses. But, as far as I am concerned, this is more beneficial to employers than workers, as they have no obligation to work on every occasion. Conversely, employers should respect their freedom and support workers during workdays. Also, workers should set boundaries for their working hours and avoid excessive workloads to keep themselves healthy. To sum up, employers should avoid contacting their workers during holiday, which can prevent them from excessive work, loads of stress and to keep close relationship with their friends.', 'Nowadays, many employees complain about the phenomenon of overwork during holidays as their employers give them work outside working hours. I strongly believe that this problem has had detrimental effects on workers, in terms of workloads, stress and social relationships. The most significant problem is that workers may suffer from higher workloads. As employers are able to connect with them at all times, they can assign duties and request work on urgent projects. As such, employees may sacrifice their holidays and take on an extra amount of work. Besides, working on holidays imposes high pressure on employees. Due to the fact that workers have to stay alert for phone calls from employers, they may devote less time to relaxation and be prepared for requests. As a result, they have less chance to relieve their stress during holidays, which may boost their anxiety under stress. Moreover, employees who need to work 24/7 may spend less time with their friends or partners. In addition to family gatherings, employees who have to stay behind at work may devote less time to social activities. This generally destroys their social bonding with peers and has negative effects on their social relationships. However, some may judge that being connected with employers enhances workers'' working efficiency. Workers who have difficulties can ask for advice from their employers and receive immediate responses. But, as far as I am concerned, this is more beneficial to employers than workers, as workers have no obligation to work on every occasion. Conversely, employers should respect workers'' freedom and support workers during workdays. Also, workers should set boundaries for their working hours and avoid excessive workloads to keep themselves healthy. To sum up, employers should avoid contacting their workers during holidays, which can protect them from excessive work and loads of stress and can help them maintain close relationships with their friends.', 310, 309, 17, 32, 'British English', 'Edmund Sir', 'approved', 1, false, false, 'Dataset 9 from the supplied Grammar Dataset 3 to 18 PDF. Approved for canonical storage in the development partition; excluded from live retrieval pending a separate production review.'
