select '2026-08-02.1', 'PARA-0013', 'POPULATION_AGE_STRUCTURES_IN_YEMEN_AND_ITALY_0013', 'Population Age Structures in Yemen and Italy', 'IELTS Academic Task 1 / Demography', 'B1–B2', 'The pie chart illustrates the age of residents of Yemen and Italy in 2000 and projections for 2050. Overall, Yemen residents is younger than Italy in 2000, this pattern keep diversifying to 2050. However, both country are projected to aged; the proportion of children will decline, while the older groups, in Italy, will become lager. In Yemen, the population of 0-14 years old is almost one-half, got 50.1%, slightly more than 15-59 years old 46.3%. By 2050, this trend will be reversed. The 15-59 age group will exceed 0-14 years old to 57.3%. The elderly population will remain comparatively small, though it is expected to increase from 3.6% to 5.7%. In Italy, the 15-59 years old age group keep being the largest slice from 2000 to 2050. Although it predicted to fall from 61.6% to 46.2%. The elderly group almost doubled from 24.1% to 42.3%. The youngster group has nearly remain unchanged through the prediction, just shrink slightly from 14.3% to 11.5%.', 'The pie chart illustrates the age distributions of the populations of Yemen and Italy in 2000 and the projected distributions for 2050. Overall, Yemen''s population was younger than Italy''s in 2000, and this contrast is expected to become more pronounced by 2050. However, both countries are projected to age; the proportion of children will decline, while the older age group in Italy will become larger. In Yemen, people aged 0–14 accounted for 50.1% of the population, slightly higher than the 46.3% recorded for those aged 15–59. By 2050, this trend will be reversed. The 15–59 age group will exceed the 0–14 age group and rise to 57.3%. The elderly population will remain comparatively small, though it is expected to increase from 3.6% to 5.7%. In Italy, the 15–59 age group is expected to remain the largest segment in both 2000 and 2050. It is predicted to fall from 61.6% to 46.2%. The proportion of elderly residents is projected to almost double from 24.1% to 42.3%. The youngest age group is projected to remain relatively stable over the period, shrinking only slightly from 14.3% to 11.5%.', 162, 185, 11, 32, 'British English', 'Edmund Sir', 'approved', 1, false, false, 'Dataset 13 from the supplied Grammar Dataset 3 to 18 PDF. Approved for canonical storage in the development partition; excluded from live retrieval pending a separate production review. Canonical word counts use whitespace-delimited tokens rather than counting percentage signs or age-range components separately.'
