select '2026-08-02.1', 'PARA-0016', 'INTERNATIONAL_STUDENT_ORIENTATION_AND_TRANSPORT_TRIAL_0016', 'International Student Orientation and Transport Trial', 'Higher Education / Transport / Student Services', 'B1–C1', 'The Eastford College launched an orientation and transport trial for international students on last autumn. During first week, students attend the university by day and return to home by the bus in the evening. They have the breakfast in their residences before taking number 12 bus. At the college, they meet in a main library rather than in the class. Students who need medical advice go to the hospital through the campus clinic, whereas those visiting a friend go to hospital as visitors. In the afternoon, some listen the radio, while others watch the television in common room. During the tour, the guide stopped explaining the ticket machine and reminded students locking their rooms. One student remembered to leave her card on a bus and tried to call the lost-property office. Replacing the card meant to complete another form, but she did not mean delaying the group. Since the trial began, the college collected feedbacks from more than two hundred students. Most of students have found the maps useful, but most of students interviewed asked for clearer fare information. A few had previously travelled alone, whereas few knew how to use the regional ticketing app. There was little of time during the first session, although tutors allowed a few extra time for questions. Each of students received a card, and all them were asked to keep it. One student said that she had gone to London last year but had never gone to Eastford before. She explained that her brother had been to Manchester and would not return until Friday. Another student apologised the tutor about arriving late and blamed the delay for a cancelled train. The tutor congratulated her for finding an alternative route and reminded to everyone that the next workshop will begin at nine. “Why the evening bus does stop so early?” one student asked. “How many routes serve the campus?” asked another. The tutor replied that nobody had complained previously, but neither the transport office had published a full timetable. One student said that he did not have some cash; another replied, “Neither I do.” The tutor added, “The app is working now, is it?” The college is also reviewing reports submitted by students who living outside the city. The reports contain several analysis of travel patterns, two serieses of photographs and three appendix. One phenomena appearing repeatedly is that students which live more far away spend lesser time on campus. The red route is more faster than the blue, while the last is more cheap. At the final meeting, the coordinator asked did the survey represented every group fair. She noted that the data was limited and that some of the evidences were inconclusive. The committee therefore requested for more informations from students which journeys involved more one form of transport. It also asked each participant to check that their address were correct. Whatever the results show, the college will continue throughout the year to provide guidance, and students will know to whom to contact when a problem will arise.', 'Eastford College launched an orientation and transport trial for international students last autumn. During the first week, students attend university by day and return home by bus in the evening. They have breakfast in their residences before taking the number 12 bus. At the college, they meet in the main library rather than in class. Students who need medical advice go to hospital through the campus clinic, whereas those visiting a friend go to the hospital as visitors. In the afternoon, some listen to the radio, while others watch television in the common room. During the tour, the guide stopped to explain the ticket machine and reminded students to lock their rooms. One student remembered leaving her card on a bus and tried calling the lost-property office. Replacing the card meant completing another form, but she did not mean to delay the group. Since the trial began, the college has collected feedback from more than two hundred students. Most students have found the maps useful, but most of the students interviewed asked for clearer fare information. A few had previously travelled alone, whereas few knew how to use the regional ticketing app. There was little time during the first session, although tutors allowed a little extra time for questions. Each of the students received a card, and all of them were asked to keep it. One student said that she had been to London the previous year but had never been to Eastford before. She explained that her brother had gone to Manchester and would not return until Friday. Another student apologised to the tutor for arriving late and blamed the delay on a cancelled train. The tutor congratulated her on finding an alternative route and reminded everyone that the next workshop would begin at nine. “Why does the evening bus stop so early?” one student asked. “How many routes serve the campus?” asked another. The tutor replied that nobody had complained previously, but neither had the transport office published a full timetable. One student said that he did not have any cash; another replied, “Neither do I.” The tutor added, “The app is working now, isn''t it?” The college is also reviewing reports submitted by students living outside the city. The reports contain several analyses of travel patterns, two series of photographs and three appendices. One phenomenon appearing repeatedly is that students who live farther away spend less time on campus. The red route is faster than the blue one, while the latter is cheaper. At the final meeting, the coordinator asked whether the survey had represented every group fairly. She noted that the data were limited and that some of the evidence was inconclusive. The committee therefore requested more information from students whose journeys involved more than one form of transport. It also asked each participant to check that their address was correct. Whatever the results show, the college will continue throughout the year to provide guidance, and students will know whom to contact when a problem arises.', 505, 500, 32, 67, 'British English', 'Edmund Sir', 'approved', 1, false, false, 'Dataset 16 from the supplied Grammar Dataset 3 to 18 PDF. Approved for canonical storage in the development partition; excluded from live retrieval pending a separate production review.'
