select '2026-08-02.1', 'PARA-0017', 'RIVERSIDE_DISTRICT_REDEVELOPMENT_0017', 'Riverside District Redevelopment', 'IELTS Academic Task 1 / Map description', 'B1–C1', 'The three maps show how Riverside district has changed from 1995 until 2025 and how it proposes to develop until 2035. Overall, the area has transformed from a lightly-developed riverside settlement to a density mixed-use neighbourhood, and the next phase intends improving pedestrian access while remain the central park. On 1995, a two-lanes road went from west towards east besides the northern bank of River Elin. A row of cottages stood in the north side of the road, opposite of a small park. The post office laid among the park and a grocery shop, while a warehouse occupied the site on the east end in the district. At south of the river, farmlands extended until the railway, and the area could only access through a narrow walking bridge. No road bridge was crossed the river, and there had no direct connection between the station to the town centre. During following thirty years, the cottages replaced by three apartment blocks, and the grocery shop was converted as a medical centre. The park was expanded to east, despite its original entrance remained at the same location. The warehouse demolished, and a supermarket constructed over its previous site. A road bridge was constructed across the river, linking between the main road and the station. In addition, the footbridge was moved for about 200 metres at the west from its original position. The road section next the park was made pedestrian, with traffic redirected in a new bypass which ran around north edge of district. A parking was added besides the supermarket, and a bus station was installed on the cross of the bypass with Station Road. Regardless these changes, the railway station remained out of the district border, at south of the new bridge. The supermarket faced to medical centre, whereas the bus stop was located diagonal opposite with the park entrance. Until 2035, it is planned a walkway for connecting the station with public square, and bicycle parkings will be supplied in the both sides of southern ramp. According to 2035 proposal, the medical centre is due for extending to north, and a pharmacy will add adjacent with it. Apartment block at centre will knock down, and its site will turn to a public square surrounding with cafés. A cycling path will run along side both river banks and pass under of the road bridge. Two ramps will provide the path an access from every side of the bridge. The existing car park will relocate underground, which allows the above land to use for a playground. In the south-east corner from the district, part of farmland will divide to plots of detached housings. These homes will be facing to the river and will connect with the station through a road branched from Station Road. At the end, the footbridge will remain as unchanged, but the neighbour riverbank will widen for creating a viewing place. By 2035, most of residents will reside at a walking distance from shops, public transports and opened spaces.', 'The three maps show how the Riverside district changed between 1995 and 2025 and how it is proposed to be developed by 2035. Overall, the area has evolved from a lightly developed riverside settlement into a denser mixed-use neighbourhood, and the next phase is intended to improve pedestrian access while retaining the central park. In 1995, a two-lane road ran from west to east along the northern bank of the River Elin. A row of cottages stood on the north side of the road, opposite a small park. The post office lay between the park and a grocery shop, while a warehouse occupied the site at the eastern end of the district. South of the river, farmland extended towards the railway, and the area was accessible only via a narrow footbridge. No road bridge crossed the river, and there was no direct connection between the station and the town centre. Over the following thirty years, the cottages were replaced by three apartment blocks, and the grocery shop was converted into a medical centre. The park was enlarged eastwards, although its original entrance remained in the same position. The warehouse was demolished, and a supermarket was built on its former site. A road bridge was constructed across the river, linking the main road with the station. In addition, the footbridge was relocated about 200 metres west of its original position. The section of road beside the park was pedestrianised, with traffic diverted onto a new bypass running around the northern edge of the district. A car park was added next to the supermarket, and a bus stop was installed at the junction of the bypass and Station Road. Despite these changes, the railway station remained outside the district boundary, south of the new bridge. The supermarket faced the medical centre, whereas the bus stop was situated diagonally opposite the park entrance. By 2035, a walkway is planned to connect the station to the public square, and bicycle parking will be provided on both sides of the southern ramp. In the 2035 proposal, the medical centre is due to be extended to the north, and a pharmacy will be added beside it. The central apartment block will be demolished, with the site becoming a public square surrounded by cafés. A cycle path will run alongside both banks of the river and pass beneath the road bridge. Two ramps will provide access to the path from either side of the bridge. The existing car park will be moved underground, allowing the land above it to be used as a playground. At the south-eastern corner of the district, part of the farmland will be divided into plots for detached houses. These homes will face the river and will be connected to the station by a road branching off Station Road. Finally, the footbridge will remain unchanged, but the nearby riverbank will be widened to create a viewing area. By 2035, most residents will live within walking distance of shops, public transport and open space.', 500, 500, 26, 99, 'British English', 'Edmund Sir', 'approved', 1, false, false, 'Dataset 17 from the supplied Grammar Dataset 3 to 18 PDF. Approved for canonical storage in the development partition; excluded from live retrieval pending a separate production review. The PDF declares 100 mappings but contains 99 distinct numbered issue rows; all 99 physically present rows are stored and no missing row was fabricated.'
