select '2026-08-02.1', 'PARA-0018', 'REGIONAL_ELECTRICITY_CONSUMPTION_AND_ENERGY_SOURCES_0018', 'Regional Electricity Consumption and Energy Sources', 'IELTS Academic Task 1 / Line graph and bar chart', 'B1–C1', 'The line graph illustrates about annual household electricity use in four regions, measured by MWh, between 2005 to 2035, while the bar chart compares between its sources. Overall, Northland led initially but Eastport overtook than it, whereas Westmere remained lowest from the four for most of period. At 2005, the figure of Northland stood 4.0 MWh, comparing with 3.2 MWh of Eastport. Northland then rose from 4.0 by a peak at 4.5 MWh in 2010, an increase by 0.5 MWh. After peaked, consumption fell of 1.1 MWh at 3.4 MWh on 2025. Until 2035, the figure is forecast to have fallen further at 3.0 MWh. Eastport had risen steadily from 3.2 to 4.4 MWh between 2005 to 2025 before reaching to 4.8 MWh in 2035. This represented a rise by 1.6 MWh, or 50 per cents. By 2020, Eastport had surpassed than Northland with 0.6 MWh. In 2035, Eastport is expected to use one and three-fifths times as much electricity than Northland. Southvale fluctuated from 2.7 and 3.1 MWh between 2010 to 2025. It bottomed at 2.7 MWh in 2015, recovered by 3.1 MWh in 2020 and levelled at 3.0 MWh. Throughout of the period, its values ranged between 2.7 to 3.3 MWh. Westmere started from 2.1 MWh and increased 1.1 MWh until 3.2 MWh. The 2035 figure is projected to be about one-and-half times than the 2005 level. Westmere remained under than Southvale until 2025 but is projected to catch it up by 2035. The bar chart presents the proportions of electricity generated from coal, gas and renewables. In 2005, coal made 45 per cent from generation, gas 37 per cent and renewables 18 per cent. Coal''s share is forecast to fall by 20 per cent, a decrease by 25 per cent points. This equals with a reduction by 56 per cent comparing with its original share. Renewables are expected to rise 18 to 47 per cent, gaining 29 per cent points. Their share will therefore be more than two and a half folds its 2005 level. Wind and solar together will be made up 47 per cent of total generation. Gas, in contrary, is projected to decline slightly, between 37 and 33 per cent. The gap among gas and renewables will shift from 19 percentage points favouring gas into 14 percentage points favouring renewables. Between the three sources, renewables will record the most large absolute increase. Coal will experience the most sharp fall between the three, while gas will remain a second largest source. The figures for coal, gas and renewables will be 20, 33 and 47 per cents, respectably. Taking together, the charts indicate convergence in consumption and a shift towards renewable generation. Comparing with 2005, the projected 2035 energy mix contains a much smaller coal share. The combined regional total is projected to raise from 12.1 to 14.3 MWh. The difference of the fastest-growing and slowest-growing regions is projected to be widened. At end of the period, Eastport will rank the first, followed with Southvale and Westmere, with Northland at last.', 'The line graph illustrates annual household electricity use in four regions, measured in MWh, between 2005 and 2035, while the bar chart compares its sources. Overall, Northland led initially but Eastport overtook it, whereas Westmere remained the lowest of the four for most of the period. In 2005, the figure for Northland stood at 4.0 MWh, compared with 3.2 MWh for Eastport. Northland then rose from 4.0 to a peak of 4.5 MWh in 2010, an increase of 0.5 MWh. After peaking, consumption fell by 1.1 MWh to 3.4 MWh in 2025. By 2035, the figure is forecast to have fallen further to 3.0 MWh. Eastport rose steadily from 3.2 to 4.4 MWh between 2005 and 2025 before reaching 4.8 MWh in 2035. This represented a rise of 1.6 MWh, or 50 per cent. By 2020, Eastport had surpassed Northland by 0.6 MWh. In 2035, Eastport is expected to use one and three-fifths times as much electricity as Northland. Southvale fluctuated between 2.7 and 3.1 MWh from 2010 to 2025. It bottomed out at 2.7 MWh in 2015, recovered to 3.1 MWh in 2020 and levelled off at 3.0 MWh. Over the period, its values ranged from 2.7 to 3.3 MWh. Westmere started at 2.1 MWh and increased by 1.1 MWh to 3.2 MWh. The 2035 figure is projected to be about one and a half times the 2005 level. Westmere remained below Southvale until 2025 but is projected to catch up with it by 2035. The bar chart presents the proportions of electricity generated from coal, gas and renewables. In 2005, coal made up 45 per cent of generation, gas 37 per cent and renewables 18 per cent. Coal''s share is forecast to fall to 20 per cent, a decrease of 25 percentage points. This is equivalent to a reduction of 56 per cent relative to its share. Renewables are expected to rise from 18 to 47 per cent, gaining 29 percentage points. Their share will therefore be more than two and a half times its 2005 level. Wind and solar together will make up 47 per cent of total generation. Gas, by contrast, is projected to decline slightly, from 37 to 33 per cent. The gap between gas and renewables will shift from 19 percentage points favouring gas to 14 percentage points favouring renewables. Of the three sources, renewables will record the largest absolute increase. Coal will experience the sharpest fall of the three, while gas will remain the second-largest source. The figures for coal, gas and renewables will be 20, 33 and 47 per cent, respectively. Taken together, the charts indicate convergence in consumption and a shift towards renewable generation. Compared with 2005, the projected 2035 energy mix contains a much smaller coal share. The combined regional total is projected to rise from 12.1 to 14.3 MWh. The difference between the fastest-growing and slowest-growing regions is projected to widen. At the end of the period, Eastport will rank first, followed by Southvale and Westmere, with Northland last.', 505, 500, 33, 75, 'British English', 'Edmund Sir', 'approved', 1, false, false, 'Dataset 18 from the supplied Grammar Dataset 3 to 18 PDF. Approved for canonical storage in the development partition; excluded from live retrieval pending a separate production review.'
